description: map root to the SYSTEM SID on a shared prefix
 The machine-level wineserver runs as root -- it has to exist before anyone
 logs in, and outlive every logout, which a session-scoped server cannot do.
 On Windows the services it hosts run as SYSTEM, so root has to *be* SYSTEM
 rather than merely be an administrator.
 .
 Without this, root gets an ordinary per-uid SID like any other user, and a
 boot-time service looks wrong to the SCM and to any security descriptor naming
 Local System -- including the one Wine itself puts in every default DACL.
 .
 Completes clause 5 of the S2 gate: one wineserver owned by root, services.exe
 running inside it, and both logged-in users seeing the same service through the
 same SCM.
origin: Stained Glass OS
bug: https://github.com/Stained-Glass-OS/stained-glass/issues/2
forwarded: not-needed
 Stained Glass OS does not submit patches upstream; see ADR 0006.
last-update: 2026-09-22

--- a/server/token.c
+++ b/server/token.c
@@ -243,7 +243,16 @@
 
 const struct sid *security_unix_uid_to_sid( uid_t uid )
 {
-    if (sg_system_prefix) return sg_sid_for_uid( uid );
+    if (sg_system_prefix)
+    {
+        /* root is SYSTEM. The machine-level wineserver runs as root and hosts
+         * the services that must exist before anyone logs in, and on Windows
+         * those run as SYSTEM -- so the mapping is not a convenience, it is
+         * what makes a boot-time service look right to the SCM and to any
+         * security descriptor naming Local System. */
+        if (uid == 0) return &local_system_sid;
+        return sg_sid_for_uid( uid );
+    }
 
     /* very simple mapping: either the current user or not the current user */
     if (uid == getuid())
